trace:
To rationalize the denominator of $\frac{1+\sqrt{3}}{1-\sqrt{3}}$, we need to multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, which is $1+\sqrt{3}$.

\begin{align*}
\frac{1+\sqrt{3}}{1-\sqrt{3}} &= \frac{(1+\sqrt{3})(1+\sqrt{3})}{(1-\sqrt{3})(1+\sqrt{3})} \\
&= \frac{1+2\sqrt{3}+3}{1-3} \\
&= \frac{4+2\sqrt{3}}{-2} \\
&= -2-\sqrt{3}
\end{align*}

Thus, in the form $A+B\sqrt{C}$, we have $A=-2$, $B=-1$, and $C=3$. Therefore, $ABC=(-2)(-1)(3)=\boxed{6}$.

